1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,950
Lesson 4: parallel dipoles

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:13,087
YA-NEC course, lesson 4: parallel dipoles - several bands on one coax. Calculators, Multiband dipole.

3
00:00:13,137 --> 00:00:24,021
Mode 'parallel rods': one dipole per band, one below the other, all on the same feed point. Here 20, 15 and 10 m, 2 mm wire, 10 cm apart, free space.

4
00:00:24,071 --> 00:00:33,253
'Tune and verify' tunes all lengths together, because each dipole detunes the others.

5
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:41,053
Results per band: 20 m 10.27 m, SWR 1.21 at band centre; 15 m 6.87 m, SWR 1.03; 10 m 5.01 m, SWR 1.19.

6
00:00:41,103 --> 00:00:51,186
The 'steals' column: on 10 m the 15 m dipole carries 72 % of the 10 m current. On 10 m the band edges reach SWR 6: the bandwidth shrinks.

7
00:00:51,236 --> 00:01:09,660
Now add 40 m and tune again.

8
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:20,194
Here is the trap of parallel dipoles: on 15 m the 40 m dipole is three half waves long and resonates too. It carries 132 % of the 15 m current.

9
00:01:20,244 --> 00:01:30,594
The 15 m SWR rises to 1.69 and the 10 m edges to 7.9. Classic remedies: a different angle for the 15 m wire, more spacing, or leave 15 m out.

10
00:01:30,644 --> 00:01:39,906
Second mode: open sleeve, as described by Tom Schiller N6BT.

11
00:01:39,956 --> 00:01:48,707
Only the 20 m dipole is fed. The 15 and 10 m elements are parasitic, very close, in the same plane: here 20 mm tubes.

12
00:01:48,757 --> 00:02:16,943
'Tune and verify' now looks for lengths and distances of the sleeves.

13
00:02:16,993 --> 00:02:25,410
Sleeves at 16 and 10 cm. SWR at band centre: 20 m 1.50, 15 m 1.09, 10 m 1.14. No coax to the parasitic elements.

14
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:35,476
Same rule as before: the bands influence each other, so the simulator tunes them together, in seconds instead of afternoons on the roof.

15
00:02:35,526 --> 00:02:41,477
Parallel dipoles and open sleeve: both from one calculator. Next lesson: traps and the trapped dipole. 73 de IC8TEM
